<title>RUSSIA: Russian T-bill tax to have little impact - minister.</title>
<headline>Russian T-bill tax to have little impact - minister.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1997-02-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Russia's new 15-percent tax on earnings from the discount on government securities will have a limited impact, Finance Minister Alexander Livshits said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>&quot;The law is unlikely to have any serious impact on the market,&quot; Livshits told a news conference. &quot;There will be a short-term splash in an unclear direction after which the market will calm down.&quot;</p>
<p>Russia's Justice Ministry approved a request from the Finance Ministry to treat earnings from the discount on GKO Treasury bills as interest, which qualifies them for a 15-percent tax rate.</p>
<p>The new law set a 15-percent tax on interest paid on bond coupons but implied that the usual profit tax rates of 35-43 percent had been extended to other earnings on government paper.</p>
<p>Dealers forecast that yields on government paper will rise in proportion to the tax rate.</p>
<p>--Julie Tolkacheva, Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
